{{Quote|You wanted to ''free'' your fellow Wookiees&mdash;well, now you can join them mining ore on Jabiim.|A First Order stormtrooper commander, to Chewbacca|<ref name="Ghosts of Kashyyyk, Part 3" />}}
'''Jabiim''' was an obscure [[planet]] in the [[Outer Rim Territories]] known mainly for its [[mining]] industry. Jabiim was the site of a [[Hidden Path]] [[Jabiim Hidden Path safehouse|safehouse]] during the [[Imperial Era]].

==Description==
Jabiim was a terrestrial [[planet]]<ref name="Part V audio">{{Kenobi|Part V}} &mdash; Audio description</ref> situated within the regions known as the [[Outer Rim Territories]]<ref name="TFABG" /> and [[the Slice]].<ref name="The Slice" /> Connected to [[Sy Myrth]] and [[Taskeed system|Taskeed]] by [[hyperspace route]]s, it was positioned at the coordinates S-7<ref name="TFABG" /> on the [[Standard Galactic Grid]].<ref name="Rebel Dossier">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Rebel Dossier]]''</ref> The world had [[ore]], which would become one of its main exports.<ref name="Ghosts of Kashyyyk, Part 3" />

==History==
===Clone Wars===
During the [[Clone Wars]] between the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems]] and [[Galactic Republic]], the Confederacy established a [[Unidentified Separatist prison camp|prison camp]] on Jabiim, where three undercover [[Intelligence officer|officers]] from [[Republic Intelligence]] came to be imprisoned among many other captives.<ref name="SWZC" />

===Imperial Era===
By the [[Imperial Era]], Jabiim was a planet that had been largely forgotten by the galaxy.<ref name="JabiimDB">{{Databank|jabiim|Jabiim}}</ref> It was only known for its [[mining]] operations and as a way station for more desirable destinations,<ref name="DoR:TVG">''[[Star Wars: Dawn of Rebellion: The Visual Guide]]''</ref> becoming a dumping ground and sanitation depot.<ref name="Designing the Galaxy">{{HomeVideoCite|set=Obi-Wan Kenobi: The Complete Series|featurette=Designing the Galaxy}}</ref> Due to this reputation,<ref name="JabiimDB" /> the [[Hidden Path]], an underground network that helped [[Force-sensitive]]s escape [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] persecution, used a [[Jabiim Hidden Path safehouse|safehouse]] on Jabiim as the penultimate destination on "the Path," in which [[Jedi]] and other Force-sensitives received new identities.<ref name="Part III">{{Kenobi|Part III}}</ref>

[[File:IntoTheBreach-OWKpV.png|thumb|left|270px|The [[Imperial Army]] attacks the Path compound on Jabiim.]]
In [[9 BBY]],<ref name="Timeline 9 BBY">{{KenobiDate|IV|the attack on Jabiim}}</ref> [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]], [[Tala Durith]], and young [[Princess]] [[Leia Skywalker Organa Solo|Leia Organa]] joined the rest of the Path in getting Leia to safety. However, Leia's companion droid, [[L0-LA59]] had been outfitted with a tracking device by the [[Reva Sevander|Third Sister]], allowing her and [[Anakin Skywalker|Darth Vader]] to follow them to the planet.<ref name="Part IV" /> During the [[Attack on Jabiim|attack]], the Path, Kenobi and Organa among them, managed to escape Vader's wrath. The Third Sister would also attempt to assassinate Vader soon after in revenge for his role in the Great Jedi Purge, but Vader humiliated the former Youngling and left her to die before revealing that the [[Grand Inquisitor]], whom she thought she had also assassinated, had survived.<ref name="Part V">{{Kenobi|Part V}}</ref>

===First Order Era===
As of [[34 ABY]],<ref name="34ABYDate">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of "[[Ghosts of Kashyyyk, Part 3]]" to [[34 ABY]].</ref> Jabiim had fallen under the control of the [[First Order]],<ref name="Ghosts of Kashyyyk, Part 3" /> the successor state to the [[Battle of Jakku|fallen]] Galactic Empire.<ref name="Encyclopedia" /> The First Order trafficked [[Wookiee]] [[Slavery|slaves]] to Jabiim from the Wookiee homeworld of [[Kashyyyk]], forcing those they brought to Jabiim to [[Mining|mine]] ore. When the [[Resistance]] operative [[Chewbacca]] was captured during the [[blockade of Kashyyyk]], a First Order [[Unidentified First Order stormtrooper commander (Kashyyyk)|stormtrooper commander]] planned to send him to Jabiim along with a group of slaves. With the assistance of a [[porg]] named [[Terbus]], however, Chewbacca managed to free these Wookiee [[prisoner]]s and escape enslavement.<ref name="Ghosts of Kashyyyk, Part 3" />

==Behind the scenes==
===Development===
{{Quote|Poor Obi-Wan always has a tough time on Jabiim.|Matt Martin|<ref name="MattTweet">{{Twitter|missingwords|status/1537143650353696768|[[Matt Martin]]|date=2022-06-15|quote=Poor Obi-Wan always has a tough time on Jabiim.}}</ref>}}
[[File:Jabiim-FoC.jpg|thumb|right|200px|Jabiim, as it appears in the ''Star Wars'' Legends continuity]]
Jabiim was first shown in the new ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]] on a [[map]] in the [[Star Wars: The Force Awakens Beginner Game|''Star Wars: The Force Awakens'' Beginner Game]], published by [[Fantasy Flight Games]] in [[2016]].<ref name="TFABG" /> It made its live-action debut in "[[Part IV]]" of the ''[[Star Wars: Obi-Wan Kenobi]]'' television series.<ref name="Part IV" /> As noted by artist [[Doug Chiang]], the team behind ''Obi-Wan Kenobi'' believed Jabiim had a very strong graphic design to it, which informed both its color palette and form as a crater. Taking inspiration from these designs, they designed Jabiim with the hope it would come across as a galactic dumping ground or sanitation depot.<ref name="Designing the Galaxy" />

In the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity, [[Jabiim/Legends|Jabiim]] first appeared in the [[Star Wars comic books|comic]] [[Republic 55|''Republic'' 55]], written by [[W. Haden Blackman]], penciled by [[Brian Ching]], and published by [[Dark Horse Comics]]<ref name="Republic 55">[[Republic 55|''Republic'' 55]]</ref> on [[July 2]], [[2003]].<ref name="DH">{{DarkHorse|url=Comics/12-201/Star-Wars-Republic-55The-Battle-of-Jabiim-Part-1-of-4|text=Star Wars: Republic #55}}</ref> In Legends, it was depicted as a planet that had sided with the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems/Legends|Confederacy of Independent Systems]] during the [[Clone Wars/Legends|Clone Wars]] between the CIS and [[Galactic Republic/Legends|Galactic Republic]]. In response, the Republic launched an invasion<ref name="Republic 55" /> in [[22 BBY/Legends|22 BBY]]<ref name="LegendsDate">''[[The Essential Reader's Companion]]'' and {{FactFile2014|30|VEN 1-6|Asajj Ventress}} establish that the Battle of Jabiim of [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wats'' Legends]] happened in the first few [[Standard month/Legends|months]] of the [[Clone Wars/Legends|Clone Wars]] and before the start of the ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'' series in 22 BBY.</ref> and kicked off the grueling [[Battle of Jabiim]], which [[Obi-Wan Kenobi/Legends|Obi-Wan Kenobi]] took part in as a [[Jedi General/Legends|Jedi General]].<ref name="Republic 55" /> Following its appearance in the ''Obi-Wan Kenobi'' series, [[Matt Martin]] of the [[Lucasfilm Story Group]] compared its role in the Legends ''Republic'' comics and canon television series by joking about how Kenobi always seemed to have a tough time when he stepped foot on Jabiim.<ref name="MattTweet" />

===''Star Wars Zero Company''===
{{Quote|GUARDS! GUARDS! THE PRISONERS ARE TRYING TO ESCAPE!|A response to two jail break outcomes|<ref name="SWZC" />}}
[[File:ZeroCompany-KeyArt.png|thumb|left|300px|''Star Wars: Zero Company'' features a mission in which the player is asked how many prisoners they are willing to risk a rescue for on Jabiim (several Zero Company operators, pictured).]]
Jabiim appears as an optional text-based mission in the [[2026]] tactics game ''[[Star Wars Zero Company]]''. As the mission is optional, this article does not assume whether or not the covert operations group [[Zero Company]] undertook the mission.<ref name="SWZC" />

The mission entails Zero Company being approached by Republic Intelligence officer [[Bennic Halloren]] to rescue the three captured Republic Intelligence operatives in the Jabiim prison camp. Should the player chose to take on the contract, Zero Company's operatives land on Jabiim and quickly manage to identify the three operatives they have been hired to rescue, but the team is also approached by prisoners willing to pay for freedom and those who simply beg for release. As such, the player is then asked whether they choose to rescue only the three Republic Intelligence officers, the officers and the several prisoners who are able to pay for rescue, or all the prisoners.<ref name="SWZC" />

If the player chooses to free only the officers, Zero Company rationalizes that anything else is too dangerous and successfully extracts the officers, who, in turn, reward Zero Company with useful intelligence. If the player chooses to save just the officers and paying customers, there is a chance they all will be extracted, in which case Zero Company is rewarded with both intelligence and [[Galactic Credit Standard|credits]]. However, there is also a chance that the guards will be alerted to the situation, possibly due to an angered prisoner who was unable to pay and wants revenge on Zero Company for rejecting their pleas. Zero Company is still able to rescue the three intelligence officers and several others, but many other prisoners were recaptured.<ref name="SWZC" />

Finally, if the player decides to save all the prisoners, Zero Company's operators decide that no one deserves to "rot" in the Confederate prison. In one outcome, Zero Company is again besieged as the guards are alerted to the situation, resulting in many prisoners being recaptured, although Zero Company does manage to extract the intelligence officers and several other prisoners. Alternatively, Zero Company might be able to liberate the entire prison camp in "a heist for the ages," after which Zero Company is handsomely rewarded. After completing the mission, there is also a chance the player will be invited to a high-stakes [[sabacc]] game by one of the liberated prisoners, who is still grateful for the rescue and offers Zero Company her seat at the tournament.<ref name="SWZC" />

